    <node id="sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._drain_body">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._drain_body</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_drain_body</data>
      <data key="d10">MCPRequestHandler._drain_body</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/http_server.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">778</data>
      <data key="d13">804</data>
      <data key="d14">def _drain_body(self) -&gt; None:</data>
      <data key="d15">Discard the request body before refusing without having read it.

        Closing a socket that still holds unread bytes makes the OS send an RST
        rather than a FIN. On Windows the client then loses the response it was
        about to read and raises `ConnectionAbortedError` (WinError 10053)
        instead -- so a refusal that goes to the trouble of naming its reason
        ("Origin not allowed") arrives as an opaque connection error. The Host
        and Origin checks run before `_read_body` by design, which is what left
        them in that position.

        Best-effort and silent</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="3349.33" y="1381.00" width="157.30" height="33.80" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">MCPRequestHand…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
