    <node id="sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._client_identity">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._client_identity</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_client_identity</data>
      <data key="d10">MCPRequestHandler._client_identity</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/http_server.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1011</data>
      <data key="d13">1027</data>
      <data key="d14">def _client_identity(self, identity: Any) -&gt; str:</data>
      <data key="d15">A stable key for rate limiting: verified subject, else network identity.

        A real OIDC subject (`sub` claim) distinguishes callers sharing one
        server; the shared static bearer token cannot distinguish its holders
        from each other by design (it names no one in particular), so every
        `bearer`-mode caller collapses to one bucket keyed on the mode itself
        -- a known, documented limitation, not an oversight. No auth
        configured at all falls back to network identity.</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="4748.30" y="1381.00" width="136.12" height="29.25" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">MCPRequestHand…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._remote_ip">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/http_server.py::MCPRequestHandler._remote_ip</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_remote_ip</data>
      <data key="d10">MCPRequestHandler._remote_ip</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/http_server.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1029</data>
      <data key="d13">1042</data>
      <data key="d14">def _remote_ip(self) -&gt; str:</data>
      <data key="d15">The caller's address, honouring `X-Forwarded-For` only when both
        `trust_proxy` is enabled AND the connecting peer is itself in
        `trusted_proxies` (#267). Neither condition alone is enough -- an
        operator that turns on `trust_proxy` without also naming which peers
        may set the header would let any direct, untrusted caller claim to be
        someone else purely by sending the header itself.</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="4908.43" y="1381.00" width="133.10" height="28.60" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">MCPRequestHand…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
