      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1144</data>
      <data key="d13">1211</data>
      <data key="d14">def _bidirectional_bfs(
    index: Index,
    from_id: str,
    to_id: str,
    wanted: frozenset,
    max_hops: int,
    visited_cap: int,
):</data>
      <data key="d15">Meet-in-the-middle BFS over index.adj, alternating the smaller frontier.

    Real work is roughly `max_hops / 2` layers deep on each side rather than
    `max_hops` deep on one -- the difference between tractable and not on a
    dense call graph (#386). Returns
    `(dist_f, parents_f, dist_b, parents_b, meet, truncated)`; `dist_f`/
    `parents_f` are rooted at `from_id`, `dist_b`/`parents_b` at `to_id`, and
    `meet` is the node where the two frontiers first touched (or None).</data>
