    </node>
    <node id="sym:repo2graph/mcp.py::_edge_note">
      <data key="d0">sym:repo2graph/mcp.py::_edge_note</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_edge_note</data>
      <data key="d10">_edge_note</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">repo2graph/mcp.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1767</data>
      <data key="d13">1795</data>
      <data key="d14">def _edge_note(index: "Index", src: str, dst: str, etype: str) -&gt; str:</data>
      <data key="d15">Where the relationship is written, and how sure repo2graph is of it.

    The node label already says where the *neighbour* is defined. For "what
    calls this", the citation an agent actually needs is the call site, which
    lives on the edge -- and without the confidence, an ambiguous name match
    reads exactly like a certain one. `compute_freshness -&gt; ResultCache.get`
    is a real example from this repository: a `.get()` on a dict resolved to
    one of three candidates at 0.5, and the tool presented it as fact.

    Certain edges get only their evidence, so the common case stays terse</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="1874.00" y="1624.60" width="103.20" height="31.20" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">_edge_note</y:NodeLabel>
