    <node id="sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_tools_list_annotations_follow_this_server_s_build_capability">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_http_transport.py::test_tools_list_annotations_follow_this_server_s_build_capability</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_tools_list_annotations_follow_this_server_s_build_capability</data>
      <data key="d10">test_tools_list_annotations_follow_this_server_s_build_capability</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_http_transport.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">1281</data>
      <data key="d13">1305</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_tools_list_annotations_follow_this_server_s_build_capability(make_server, tmp_path):</data>
      <data key="d15">#292: `readOnlyHint` must describe the server answering, not the tool.

    `tools/list` is answered once for a server's whole lifetime, so the
    annotation has to reflect whether *this* server can build. A server given
    a repo to build from may, on the first call to any tool but
    `repo_build_status`, parse the whole repository, run git and write
    `.r2g/**` -- and the client inspecting annotations has no other way to
    learn that.

    Detector: this handler used to spread the flat `TOOL_ANNOTATIONS`
    constant, so the second half of this test saw `readOnlyHint: True`.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="329.32" y="3745.55" width="136.12" height="29.25" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_tools_lis…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
