    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::_js_regex">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::_js_regex</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">_js_regex</data>
      <data key="d10">_js_regex</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">380</data>
      <data key="d13">391</data>
      <data key="d14">def _js_regex(content: str, name: str, arg: str) -&gt; re.Pattern:</data>
      <data key="d15">Pull a `const &lt;name&gt; = /&lt;pattern&gt;/i.test(&lt;arg&gt;)` literal out of the script.

    The retarget guard is the one piece of prod-igy whose *behaviour* is worth
    asserting rather than its source text, and the pytest environment has no
    Node (see this module's docstring). Extracting the literal and compiling it
    with `re` tests the pattern itself: a typo in the login shape fails here
    instead of silently retargeting a release PR in production.</data>
