    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_ai_text_strips_markdown_images_and_links">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_ai_text_strips_markdown_images_and_links</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_ai_text_strips_markdown_images_and_links</data>
      <data key="d10">test_ai_text_strips_markdown_images_and_links</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">647</data>
      <data key="d13">718</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_ai_text_strips_markdown_images_and_links():</data>
      <data key="d15">#373: markdown image/link syntax must not survive into the comment.

    `![](url)` is fetched server-side by GitHub's camo proxy the moment the
    comment renders -- a request to an attacker-controlled host that confirms
    the workflow ran and when. `[text](url)`, a reference-style `[label]: url`
    definition, an autolink, and an HTML `&lt;img&gt;` are all the same
    reviewer-phishing primitive under a trusted bot identity. Adversarial
    inputs: reference-style links, nested brackets, `![]()`, autolinks, HTML
    `&lt;img&gt;`, and a URL that smuggles a token in its query string.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="833.90" y="4569.85" width="139.15" height="29.90" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_ai_text_s…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_ai_text_neutralises_protocol_relative_and_entity_encoded_links">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_ai_text_neutralises_protocol_relative_and_entity_encoded_links</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_ai_text_neutralises_protocol_relative_and_entity_encoded_links</data>
      <data key="d10">test_ai_text_neutralises_protocol_relative_and_entity_encoded_links</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">721</data>
      <data key="d13">757</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_ai_text_neutralises_protocol_relative_and_entity_encoded_links():</data>
      <data key="d15">Audit round 1: three forms the https-only / tag-allowlist rules missed.

    - `&lt;a href="//evil.example/login"&gt;`: raw HTML with a protocol-relative URL;
    - `[r]: //evil.example/x`: a reference definition with no scheme at all;
    - `&lt;a href="HTTPS&amp;#58;//evil.example"&gt;`: the scheme's colon as an entity,
      which the renderer decodes *after* any regex has looked at the text.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="997.05" y="4569.85" width="148.23" height="31.85" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_ai_text_n…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_summary_step_does_exactly_two_things">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_summary_step_does_exactly_two_things</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_summary_step_does_exactly_two_things</data>
      <data key="d10">test_summary_step_does_exactly_two_things</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">760</data>
      <data key="d13">792</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_summary_step_does_exactly_two_things():</data>
      <data key="d15">The schema is the whole contract, and it has two fields.

    Labels stay path- and size-derived: `detectType`, `detectAreas` and
    `calculateSize` are exact, and a model guess that contradicted them would
    make a label mean "computed" on some PRs and "guessed" on others with
    nothing in the comment to say which. Review, risk assessment and anything
    requiring knowledge of code outside the diff are out of scope by
    construction -- there is no field to put them in.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
      <data key="d_nodegraphics">
        <y:ShapeNode>
          <y:Geometry x="1169.28" y="4569.85" width="154.27" height="33.15" />
          <y:Fill color="#57C7E3" transparent="false" />
          <y:BorderStyle color="#4a4f57" type="line" width="1.0" />
          <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" fontSize="11" textColor="#1c2330" visible="true">test_summary_s…</y:NodeLabel>
          <y:Shape type="ellipse" />
        </y:ShapeNode>
      </data>
    </node>
    <node id="sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_summary_step_is_not_shown_the_codebase">
      <data key="d0">sym:tests/test_prod_igy.py::test_summary_step_is_not_shown_the_codebase</data>
      <data key="d1">symbol</data>
      <data key="d2">test_summary_step_is_not_shown_the_codebase</data>
      <data key="d10">test_summary_step_is_not_shown_the_codebase</data>
      <data key="d11">function</data>
      <data key="d3">tests/test_prod_igy.py</data>
      <data key="d4">python</data>
      <data key="d12">795</data>
      <data key="d13">822</data>
      <data key="d14">def test_summary_step_is_not_shown_the_codebase():</data>
      <data key="d15">It sees one diff. It is not given repository rules or any other file.

    Feeding AGENTS.md (or any repo content) to the model is what turns a
    description into a review, which is the thing this bot deliberately does
    not do. The rule checklist in the comment stays where it was: derived from
    changed paths by `checkAgentsRules`, with no model involved.</data>
      <data key="d16">true</data>
